[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"SOLID Principles in Java Design"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"The SOLID principles are a set of five design principles intended to make software designs more understandable, flexible, and maintainable. Originally introduced by Robert C. Martin, these principles form the bedrock of robust object-oriented programming in Java."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A class should have one, and only one, reason to change. This means a class should have only one job or responsibility, leading to higher cohesion and lower coupling."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"public class InvoicePrinter {
    public void print(Invoice invoice) {
        System.out.println("Printing invoice: " + invoice.getId());
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Open/Closed Principle (OCP)"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Software entities should be open for extension, but closed for modification. You should be able to add new functionality without changing existing, well-tested code by utilizing interfaces or abstract classes."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"public interface DiscountCalculator {
    double calculate(double amount);
}

public class VipDiscount implements DiscountCalculator {
    @Override
    public double calculate(double amount) {
        return amount * 0.8;
    }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Derived classes must be substitutable for their base classes without altering the correct behavior of the system. If class B is a subtype of class A, then objects of type A may be replaced with objects of type B without breaking the application."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"A client should never be forced to implement an interface that it does not use, or methods it does not need. Instead of one fat interface, many small interfaces are preferred based on groups of methods."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"public interface Workable {
    void work();
}

public interface Eatable {
    void eat();
}

public class Worker implements Workable, Eatable {
    public void work() { /* Working... */ }
    public void eat() { /* Eating... */ }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"5. Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules. Both should depend on abstractions. Furthermore, abstractions should not depend on details; details should depend on abstractions."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"public interface MessageSender {
    void sendMessage(String message);
}

public class NotificationService {
    private final MessageSender sender;

    public NotificationService(MessageSender sender) {
        this.sender = sender;
    }

    public void notifyUser(String msg) {
        sender.sendMessage(msg);
    }
}"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"SRP keeps classes small and focused."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"OCP ensures safe extensibility."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"LSP guarantees predictable polymorphism."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"ISP prevents bloated interfaces."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"DIP decouples systems via dependency injection."}]
